<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Love song · c. 1170 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Gita Govinda</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="sa">गीतगोविन्दम्</bdi> / <bdi lang="sa-Latn">Gītagovindam</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Sanskrit song-poem from the 1100s about the god Krishna and Radha, still sung every night in a great temple at Puri.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Jayadeva</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Jayadeva (ragas and talas indicated by the poet)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Hindu devotional (Vaishnava) tradition of Odisha and India</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Odisha, India (Kenduli, by the tradition Jones recorded; disputed)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>12th century; earliest commentary c. 1190</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by Jayadeva (12th century))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gita-govinda">Gita Govinda</a> is a work of songs composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> by the 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a>. It tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Radha and the gopis, the cowherd women of Vrindavan: Krishna's love for Radha, his straying and his return to her, a story read as the human soul turning away from God and coming back. The poem has twelve chapters, each named for a mood of Krishna, from exuberant to exultant, divided into twenty-four prabandhas; the songs within them are ashtapadis, groups of eight couplets. It also sets out the eight moods of the heroine, the Ashta Nayika.</p><p>It is one of the earliest musical texts in which the author names the raga (mode) and tala (rhythm) for every song. Most of the ragas and talas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a> indicated, such as Mangala Gujjari and the āṭhatāli rhythm, are unique to the tradition of Odissi music and have stayed in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> for centuries. The work was known at Puri soon after it was written: the earliest commentary, Udayanacharya's Bhavavibhavini Tika, was written in Odisha around 1190. Many more followed, among them the Rasikapriya of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> and, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudiya-vaishnavism">Gaudiya Vaishnava</a> school, the commentaries of Prabhodananda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Sarasvati</a> and of Chaitanyadasa.</p><p>At the Jagannatha temple of Puri the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gita-govinda">Gita Govinda</a> is sung every night during the Badasinghara, the temple's last ritual of the day, to traditional Odissi ragas and talas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a> himself is said to have sung in the temple, and the tradition has continued unbroken since his time. After him its singing became a required temple service performed by the Maharis or Devadasis, recorded in inscriptions, in the temple chronicle Mādalā Pānji and in other official documents. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> musicians adapted the ashtapadis into sopana sangeetham, a form sung in temples, and the poem's moods of the heroine have inspired many compositions and dances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s classical dance traditions.</p><p>The poem became known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through Sir William Jones's English translation, published in the Transactions of the Asiatic Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1792, which named Kenduli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> (ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>) as the poet's birthplace. F. H. van Dalberg's German version, made from Jones, was read by Goethe, and Friedrich Rückert began a verse translation in 1829. Later English versions include Edwin Arnold's The Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a> (1875) and Barbara Stoler Miller's Love <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Dark Lord (1977). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">Miniature paintings</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gita-govinda">Gita Govinda</a> are held in museums from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhubaneswar">Bhubaneswar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, Zürich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A cycle of songs on the love of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Radha: their longing, separation, jealousy and reunion, told through the moods of the lovers and of Radha's companion. Devotees read the lovers' parting and return as the soul's separation from God and its return. The mood moves from yearning to joy.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Sanskrit</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Sanskrit</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">प्रलयपयोधिजले धृतवानसि वेदम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">pralayapayodhijale dhṛtavānasi vedam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the waters of the ocean of the world's dissolution you held the Veda,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">विहितवहित्रचरित्रमखेदम् ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vihitavahitracaritramakhedam ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>taking on, unwearied, the part of a ship.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतमीनशरीर जय जगदीश हरे ॥ १ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtamīnaśarīra jaya jagadīśa hare || 1 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took the body of a fish, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (1)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">क्षितिरतिविपुलतरे तव तिष्ठति पृष्ठे ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kṣitirativipulatare tava tiṣṭhati pṛṣṭhe |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The earth stands on your back, vast beyond measure,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">धरणिधरणकिणचक्रगरिष्ठे ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">dharaṇidharaṇakiṇacakragariṣṭhe ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>made heaviest by the ring of scars from bearing the earth.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतकच्छपरूप जय जगदीश हरे ॥ २ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtakacchaparūpa jaya jagadīśa hare || 2 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took the form of a tortoise, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (2)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">वसति दशनशिखरे धरणी तव लग्ना ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vasati daśanaśikhare dharaṇī tava lagnā |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The earth dwells on the tip of your tusk, clinging there,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">शशिनि कलङ्ककलेव निमग्ना ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">śaśini kalaṅkakaleva nimagnā ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>like the streak of the stain sunk into the moon.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतसूकररूप जय जगदीश हरे ॥ ३ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtasūkararūpa jaya jagadīśa hare || 3 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took the form of a boar, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (3)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तव करकमलवरे नखमद्भुतशृङ्गम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tava karakamalavare nakhamadbhutaśṛṅgam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On your fine lotus hand the nail is a wondrous horn</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">दलितहिरण्यकशिपुतनुभृङ्गम् ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">dalitahiraṇyakaśiputanubhṛṅgam ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that has torn the bee that was Hiraṇyakaśipu's body.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतनरहरिरूप जय जगदीश हरे ॥ ४ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtanaraharirūpa jaya jagadīśa hare || 4 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took the form of the man-lion, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (4)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">छलयसि विक्रमणे बलिमद्भुतवामन ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">chalayasi vikramaṇe balimadbhutavāmana |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With your stride you trick Bali, wondrous dwarf,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">पदनखनीरजनितजनपावन ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">padanakhanīrajanitajanapāvana ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>purifier of people by the water born from your toenail.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतवामनरूप जय जगदीश हरे ॥ ५ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtavāmanarūpa jaya jagadīśa hare || 5 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took the form of the dwarf, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (5)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">क्षत्रियरुधिरमये जगदपगतपापम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kṣatriyarudhiramaye jagadapagatapāpam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In water made of the blood of kṣatriyas the world, its sin removed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">स्नपयसि पयसि शमितभवतापम् ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">snapayasi payasi śamitabhavatāpam ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you bathe, and the burning pain of existence is quenched.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतभृघुपतिरूप जय जगदीश हरे ॥ ६ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtabhṛghupatirūpa jaya jagadīśa hare || 6 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took the form of the lord of the Bhṛgus, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (6)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">वितरसि दिक्षु रणे दिक्पतिकमनीयम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vitarasi dikṣu raṇe dikpatikamanīyam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In battle you scatter to the quarters, pleasing to the guardians of the quarters,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">दशमुखमौलिबलिं रमणीयं ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">daśamukhamaulibaliṃ ramaṇīyaṃ ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a lovely offering of the heads of the Ten-faced one.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतरामशरीर जय जगदीश हरे ॥ ७ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtarāmaśarīra jaya jagadīśa hare || 7 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took the body of Rāma, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (7)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">वहसि वपुषि विशदे वसनं जलदाभम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vahasi vapuṣi viśade vasanaṃ jaladābham |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On your bright body you wear a garment like a rain cloud,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">हलहतिभीतिमिलितयमुनाभम् ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">halahatibhītimilitayamunābham ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the colour of the Yamunā, come together in fear of the plough's blow.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतहलधररूप जय जगदीश हरे ॥ ८ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtahaladhararūpa jaya jagadīśa hare || 8 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took the form of the plough-bearer, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (8)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">निन्दसि यज्ञविधेरहह श्रुतिजातम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nindasi yajñavidherahaha śrutijātam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! you censure the whole of the scripture's rule of sacrifice,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सदयहृदयदर्शितपशुघातम् ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">sadayahṛdayadarśitapaśughātam ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O compassionate heart, that shows the slaughter of animals.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतबुद्धशरीर जय जगदीश हरे ॥ ९ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtabuddhaśarīra jaya jagadīśa hare || 9 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took the body of the Buddha, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (9)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">म्लेच्छनिवहनिधने कलयसि करवालम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">mlecchanivahanidhane kalayasi karavālam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To destroy the hordes of the mlecchas you wield a sword,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">धूमकेतुमिव किमपि करालम् ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">dhūmaketumiva kimapi karālam ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>like a comet, something terrible.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतकल्किशरीर जय जगदीश हरे ॥ १० ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtakalkiśarīra jaya jagadīśa hare || 10 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took the body of Kalki, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (10)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">श्रीजयदेवकवेरिदमुदितमुदारम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">śrījayadevakaveridamuditamudāram |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This noble song spoken by the poet Śrī Jayadeva,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">शृणु सुखदं शुभदं भवसारम् ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">śṛṇu sukhadaṃ śubhadaṃ bhavasāram ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>hear it: it gives joy, it gives good, it is the essence of existence.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">केशव धृतदशविधरूप जय जगदीश हरे ॥ ११ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">keśava dhṛtadaśavidharūpa jaya jagadīśa hare || 11 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keśava, who took forms of ten kinds, victory, Lord of the world, Hari! (11)</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Jayadeva, Gita Govinda (12th century), first canto: the song of the ten avatars. Text from <a href="https://sa.wikisource.org/wiki/%E0%A4%97%E0%A5%80%E0%A4%A4%E0%A4%97%E0%A5%8B%E0%A4%B5%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%A8%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%A6%E0%A4%AE%E0%A5%8D%2F%E0%A4%B8%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%AE%E0%A5%8B%E0%A4%A6%E0%A4%A6%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%AE%E0%A5%8B%E0%A4%A6%E0%A4%B0%E0%A4%83" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (IAST).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (13)</summary><ul><li>line 6.3: the text has धृतभृघुपति (bhṛghu-, with gh), a slip for भृगुपति bhṛgupati, 'lord of the Bhṛgus', i.e. Paraśurāma. Romanized as written; the editor may correct orig and rom to bhṛgu-.</li><li>refrain: 'jaya jagadīśa hare' is 'be victorious (victory to you), Lord of the world, Hari!'; Keśava and Hari are names of Viṣṇu-Kṛṣṇa. The verse numbers of the original are kept in parentheses in en and as || n || in rom.</li><li>Romanization keeps the sandhi joins of the written text (dhṛtavānasi, nakhamadbhuta…), with spaces only where the Devanagari has them.</li><li>stanza 1: the fish (Matsya) rescues the Veda from the flood at the end of an age, acting as a boat.</li><li>line 3.2: the earth on the boar's tusk is likened to the dark mark (kalaṅka) on the moon.</li><li>line 4.2: Hiraṇyakaśipu's body likened to a bee crushed between the nails.</li><li>line 5.2: the water born from the dwarf's (Vāmana / Trivikrama's) toenail is the Ganges.</li><li>line 6.1-6.2: Paraśurāma, who destroyed the kṣatriyas, bathes the world in their blood; 'payasi' (in water) stands in the second line. The sense of the compound order is mine: 'the world, its sin removed' and 'the pain of existence quenched' both describe the world.</li><li>line 7.2: the Ten-faced one is Rāvaṇa; his heads are thrown to the ten directions as an offering (bali) to the guardians of the directions.</li><li>line 8.2: Balarāma (the plough-bearer, fair-skinned, dressed in dark blue) drags the Yamunā with his plough; the river, fearing his plough, comes to him, and her dark colour is his garment's.</li><li>line 9.1-9.2: disputed construction. darśita-paśughātam most naturally describes śrutijātam (the body of scripture 'in which the killing of animals is shown / taught'); sadayahṛdaya is then a vocative, 'O you of compassionate heart'. Some read the compound as 'with a compassionate heart that has seen (the evil of) animal slaughter'.</li><li>line 10.1: mleccha, a Sanskrit term for foreigners and outsiders to Vedic society ('barbarians'); kept untranslated.</li><li>line 10.2: kim api karālam, 'something terrible beyond words'.</li></ul></details></section>
